  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and humid conditions expected this week, peaking \nWednesday and Thursday. A Heat Advisory has been issued for portions \nof the area Wednesday. Isolated storms are also possible, but most \nlocations are likely to stay dry. \n\nA late summer (or early fall, depending on what definition you \nprefer) heat wave remains on tap for the upcoming week. Currently \nseeing an early taste this afternoon with temps well into the\n90s. Dew points are attempting to mix out into the 60s, so it \ndoesn't feel too humid out despite the hot temps. Still expect \nthe peak of the heat Wednesday and Thursday as the center of the\nupper-level ridge nudges east toward the TN Valley. There \nremains some uncertainty regarding a backdoor front later \nWednesday afternoon which could temper the higher-end heat \npotential further E and especially along the immediate coast and\nthe Eastern Shore. This would bring some onshore flow to these \nareas in the afternoon. Away from here, peak heat indices of \naround 105 degrees are expected by the late morning and \nafternoon. Therefore, a Heat Advisory has been issued for \nportions of central and eastern VA and northeast NC, minus those\ncloser to the coast. That boundary lifts back N on Thursday and\nthe sfc flow is likely to be S-SW for all the area, allowing \nfor widespread 105+ heat indices. In fact, some areas in SE VA \ncould near 110 F. A Heat Advisory is expected for the vast \nmajority of our CWA and will likely issue this either tonight or\ntomorrow. \n\nRegarding thunderstorm chances, isolated convection is expected both \ntoday and Wednesday. The 12z CAMs as a whole are not all that \nexcited about either day, though areas across the N and \nespecially NE (Eastern Shore, etc.) are most favored for any \nconvection. Some of the CAMs and global models depict some \nstronger convection, perhaps in the form of linear segments, to \nthe north of the local FA. It is not outside the realm of \npossibility that those storms dive south into the local area. \nNorthwest flow aloft and strong instability means that any \nstorms that do form will have the potential for strong winds and\nlarge hail. SPC has maintained the Marginal Risk for NE \nportions of the area today and tomorrow. Tomorrow's slight risk \ndoes clip northern portions of the area and was actually \nexpanded a bit southward, though this risk remains highly \nconditional on the actual presence of storms. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower/storm chances increase late week as a cold \nfront approaches. Seasonable temps forecast behind the front for the\nlater half of the Labor Day weekend.\n\nThe strong ridge aloft starts to break down/flatten late in the \nweek. Meanwhile, a sfc cold front will approach and pass through the \narea sometime Friday, likely late. An initial round of storms will \nbe possible Thursday, but coverage still looks on the low side and \nthese chances ultimately depend on where that backdoor front is \nsituated. The most favorable overlap of forcing, shear, and \ninstability is expected Friday. At the surface, the aforementioned \nfront to our N will finally make appreciable progress and descend \ninto our local area. Aloft, NW flow will remain in place and \nprogressively strengthen as the associated trough drops southward \nalong the NE/Mid-Atlantic coast, increasing the deep-layer shear. \nThis all suggests a bonafide severe wx threat for the majority of \nthe area Friday, with the potential for several fast-moving clusters \ncapable of widespread damaging winds, along with isolated supercells \nand large hail. SPC has maintained a slight risk for severe for the \narea. The risk for storms (and any potential svr wx) Saturday is \ndependent on where the front is situated during peak heating. PoPs \nfrom the NBM have increased compared to the previous forecast and \nthis is likely due to the slower trend in the ECMWF and EPS. \n\nA gradual relief from the heat begins Saturday and especially \nSunday- Monday. Storm chances also decrease as strong high \npressure builds in from the north. A first glance at the later \nhalf of the Labor Day weekend looks promising for those wanting \nfavorable wx for outdoor activities.",
